          "excerpt": "A concrete public marker of this aspiration is Broadcom’s AI opportunity. In 2024, management said its fiscal 2027 serviceable addressable market for AI accelerators and networking across its current hyperscale engagements could reach $60 billion to $90 billion. That is an opportunity target, not an achieved result. More broadly, Broadcom defines winning through a combination of category leadership in selected niches, higher recurring revenue, strong margins, and disciplined returns on capital.\n\n### 1b. Where does Broadcom play? [...] | Industry or industries | Semiconductors, infrastructure software, enterprise technology |\n| Key products or services | Custom AI accelerators, Ethernet switching and routing silicon, broadband and wireless chips, storage connectivity, VMware virtualization and private cloud software, mainframe software, enterprise cybersecurity, network and application management software |\n| Geographic footprint | Global customer base with engineering, sales, and support operations across North America, Asia-Pacific, and Europe; outsourced semiconductor manufacturing and assembly largely concentrated in Asia |\n| Business segments as officially reported | Semiconductor Solutions; Infrastructure Software |\n| Company website |  |\n![Umbrex Logo](https://umbrex.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/02/umbrex-logo.png)\n![Umbrex Logo](https://umbrex.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/02/umbrex-logo.png)\n\n[Company Profiles](https://umbrex.com/resources/company-profiles/) > Broadcom S",
          "page_text": "A concrete public marker of this aspiration is Broadcom’s AI opportunity. In 2024, management said its fiscal 2027 serviceable addressable market for AI accelerators and networking across its current hyperscale engagements could reach $60 billion to $90 billion. That is an opportunity target, not an achieved result. More broadly, Broadcom defines winning through a combination of category leadership in selected niches, higher recurring revenue, strong margins, and disciplined returns on capital.\n\n### 1b. Where does Broadcom play? [...] | Industry or industries | Semiconductors, infrastructure software, enterprise technology |\n| Key products or services | Custom AI accelerators, Ethernet switching and routing silicon, broadband and wireless chips, storage connectivity, VMware virtualization and private cloud software, mainframe software, enterprise cybersecurity, network and application management software |\n| Geographic footprint | Global customer base with engineering, sales, and support operations across North America, Asia-Pacific, and Europe; outsourced semiconductor manufacturing and assembly largely concentrated in Asia |\n| Business segments as officially reported | Semiconductor Solutions; Infrastructure Software |\n| Company website |  |\n![Umbrex Logo](https://umbrex.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/02/umbrex-logo.png)\n![Umbrex Logo](https://umbrex.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/02/umbrex-logo.png)\n\n[Company Profiles](https://umbrex.com/resources/company-profiles/) > Broadcom Strategy and Business Model\n\n# Broadcom Strategy and Business Model\n\n## Executive Overview\n\nBroadcom is a global semiconductor and infrastructure software company whose current form was created when Avago acquired Broadcom Corporation in 2016 and later adopted the Broadcom name. Headquartered in Palo Alto, California, Broadcom sells mission-critical technology into data centers, enterprise IT, broadband and telecom networks, smartphones, storage systems, and industrial markets. Its semiconductor portfolio spans custom artificial intelligence (AI) accelerators, Ethernet switching and routing silicon, broadband access chips, radio-frequency and wireless connectivity components, and storage connectivity products. Its software portfolio includes VMware virtualization and private-cloud platforms, mainframe software, cybersecurity, and application and network management products.\n\nBroadcom’s strategy is unusually focused. Rather than trying to cover all of technology, it prefers a limited set of infrastructure layers where deep engineering, large design wins, switching costs, and disciplined cost control can support strong margins and cash generation. The company serves customers globally, with outsourced semiconductor manufacturing concentrated in Asia and software customers distributed across the Americas, Europe, and Asia-Pacific. For fiscal 2024, ended November 3, 2024, Broadcom reported revenue of $51.6 billion, with the VMware acquisition making infrastructure software a much larger part of the mix and increasing the share of recurring revenue.\n\n## Broadcom at a Glance\n\n|  |  |\n| --- | --- |\n| Logo | Broadcom Logo |\n| Common name | Broadcom |\n| Full legal name | Broadcom Inc. |\n| Headquarters | [Palo Alto](https://umbrex.com/locations/north-america/united-states/california/silicon-valley/), California, United States |\n| Ownership | Public company; no controlling shareholder publicly disclosed |\n| Ticker | AVGO |\n| Exchange | NASDAQ |\n| Market Cap | $1.81T |\n| Revenue (FY2024) | $51.57B |\n| Founding / major historical milestones | Roots in the Hewlett-Packard semiconductor business in 1961; 1999 Agilent spin; 2005 sale of the chip business and formation of Avago; 2009 Avago initial public offering; 2016 Avago acquired Broadcom Corporation and adopted the Broadcom name; 2018 redomiciled to the United States as Broadcom Inc.; 2023 closed the VMware acquisition |\n| Industry or industries | Semiconductors, infrastructure software, enterprise technology |\n| Key products or services | Custom AI accelerators, Ethernet switching and routing silicon, broadband and wireless chips, storage connectivity, VMware virtualization and private cloud software, mainframe software, enterprise cybersecurity, network and application management software |\n| Geographic footprint | Global customer base with engineering, sales, and support operations across North America, Asia-Pacific, and Europe; outsourced semiconductor manufacturing and assembly largely concentrated in Asia |\n| Business segments as officially reported | Semiconductor Solutions; Infrastructure Software |\n| Company website | <https://www.broadcom.com> |\n\n![Broadcom Logo](https://umbrex.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Broadcom-Logo.png)\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n## 1. What Is the Strategy of Broadcom?\n\nBroadcom’s public statements point to a consistent strategic logic: concentrate on a relatively small number of infrastructure technology franchises where technical complexity, customer dependence, and portfolio discipline can support durable economics. Using the *Playing to Win* framework, Broadcom’s strategy can be described as follows.\n\n### 1a. What is the winning aspiration of Broadcom?\n\nBroadcom’s winning aspiration is not to be the broadest technology company. It is to own critical infrastructure layers where customers care deeply about performance, reliability, integration, and continuity, and where that importance translates into pricing power and cash generation. In semiconductors, management has emphasized leadership in focused franchises such as AI infrastructure, networking, broadband connectivity, wireless components, and storage connectivity. In infrastructure software, the company aims to control mission-critical software used to run data centers, private clouds, and mainframe environments while increasing the share of recurring revenue.\n\nA concrete public marker of this aspiration is Broadcom’s AI opportunity. In 2024, management said its fiscal 2027 serviceable addres",
