          "extracted_fact": "Regulations are the means by which Federal agencies implement and enforce the laws and legislation passed by Congress.",
          "excerpt": "U.S. Federal Regulations - United States Federal Government Resources - Library Guides at University of Washington Libraries\nSkip to Main Content\nMake a Gift\nYour Library Account\nAccount\nAsk Us!\nResearch Guides\nHome\nSources by Subject\nResearch Basics\nToggle Dropdown\nA Civics Refresher\nFinding Books & Articles\nFreedom of Information Act\nExecutive Branch\nToggle Dropdown\nThe Presidency\nThe Executive Office of the President\nDepartments and Agencies\nU.S. Federal Regulations\nExecutive Branch Documents\nFederal Register\nFreedom of Information Act (FOIA)\nOpen Government\nCongress\nToggle Dropdown\nAll Things Congressional\nLegislative Branch Overview\nU.S. Laws\nJudicial Branch\nToggle Dropdown\nU.S. Supreme Court\nLower Federal Courts\nU.S. Laws\nU.S. Data & Statistics\nToggle Dropdown\nStatistical Abstract of the United States\nU.S. Census Home\nToggle Dropdown\nCensus 2020\nCensus 2010\nCensus 2000\nEnumeration Districts\nConstitution Reading\nBack to Government Publications guide\nLibraries Home\nResearch Guides\nUniversity of Washington Libraries\nLibrary Guides\nUW Libraries\nUnited States Federal Government Resources\nU.S. Federal Regulations\nSearch this Guide\nSearch\nUnited States Federal Government Resources: U.S. Federal Regulations\nIntroduction\nA regulation is a \"general statement,\" issued by an executive agency, that has the force and effect of law. Congress often grants agencies the authority to, and sometimes requires that agencies, issue regulations. Regulations are the means by which Federal agenc",
          "page_text": "U.S. Federal Regulations - United States Federal Government Resources - Library Guides at University of Washington Libraries\nSkip to Main Content\nMake a Gift\nYour Library Account\nAccount\nAsk Us!\nResearch Guides\nHome\nSources by Subject\nResearch Basics\nToggle Dropdown\nA Civics Refresher\nFinding Books & Articles\nFreedom of Information Act\nExecutive Branch\nToggle Dropdown\nThe Presidency\nThe Executive Office of the President\nDepartments and Agencies\nU.S. Federal Regulations\nExecutive Branch Documents\nFederal Register\nFreedom of Information Act (FOIA)\nOpen Government\nCongress\nToggle Dropdown\nAll Things Congressional\nLegislative Branch Overview\nU.S. Laws\nJudicial Branch\nToggle Dropdown\nU.S. Supreme Court\nLower Federal Courts\nU.S. Laws\nU.S. Data & Statistics\nToggle Dropdown\nStatistical Abstract of the United States\nU.S. Census Home\nToggle Dropdown\nCensus 2020\nCensus 2010\nCensus 2000\nEnumeration Districts\nConstitution Reading\nBack to Government Publications guide\nLibraries Home\nResearch Guides\nUniversity of Washington Libraries\nLibrary Guides\nUW Libraries\nUnited States Federal Government Resources\nU.S. Federal Regulations\nSearch this Guide\nSearch\nUnited States Federal Government Resources: U.S. Federal Regulations\nIntroduction\nA regulation is a \"general statement,\" issued by an executive agency, that has the force and effect of law. Congress often grants agencies the authority to, and sometimes requires that agencies, issue regulations. Regulations are the means by which Federal agencies implement and enforce the laws and legislation passed by Congress. Federal regulations are created through a process known as \"rulemaking.\" Regulations effect our everyday life!\nRegulations are published in the Federal Register as they are passed. So, some regulations found in older issues of the Federal Register are not currently enforced. The regulations currently in effect at any given time are codified in the Code of Federal Regulations, which is published annually.\nLocating Federal Regulations\nCFR and Federal Register - 1938-present - HEIN Online\nThis link opens in a new window\nThis link opens in a new window\nThis collection contains the Federal Register from its inception in 1936, the Code of Federal Regulations, the U.S. Government Manual, the Weekly Compilation of Presidential Documents and more.\nCode of Federal Regulations\nThis link opens in a new window\nA sample catalog search in UW WorldCat showing individual titles.\nCode of Federal Regulations, 1996-present -- Hathi Trust\nThis link opens in a new window\nCode of Federal Regulations, Index and Finding Aids, Hathi Trust\nThis link opens in a new window\nAlso available in\nprint\n.\nCode of Federal Regulations - 1996-present - govinfo\nThis link opens in a new window\nThe CFR is the codification of the general and permanent rules published in the Federal Register by the departments and agencies of the Federal Government.\nFederal Register, Gallagher Library\nThis link opens in a new window\nPrint from 1936-.\nFederal Register, UW Libraries\nThis link opens in a new window\nPrint and microfiche holdings.\nWestlaw Campus Research\nThis link opens in a new window\nThis link opens in a new window\nClick on Federal Materials tab, then Federal Register.\n[What is the] Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), National Archives\nThis link opens in a new window\nInformation about the CFR; list of CFR subjects arranged by title; laws that affect publication; tutorials and more. . .\nProQuest Regulatory Insight\nThis link opens in a new window\nThis link opens in a new window\nU.S. federal administrative law histories beginning in 1936, organized by federal statute and Executive Order. Includes Guidance and Policy documents.\nParticipating in the Federal Regulatory Process\nRegInfo.gov\nThis link opens in a new window\nRegInfo.gov is a United States Government website produced by the Office of Management and Budget and the General Services Administration. Reginfo.gov provides reliable, transparent information about regulations under development to enable the public to participate effectively in the regulatory process.\nRegulations.gov\nThis link opens in a new window\nRegulations.gov is another source for accessing U.S. government regulations. On this site, you can: search for a proposed rule, final rule or Federal Register notice; comment on a regulation; subscribe to RSS feeds of newly posted Federal Register notices; and more!\n\"The Reg Map\"\nThe Reg Map\nis a chart that gives an overview of the \"informal rulemaking\" process. It was created by ICF Consulting with the cooperation of the U.S. General Services Administration's Regulatory Information Service Center. It's a helpful guide for understanding the regulatory process!\nRelated\nU.S. Laws\nThis link opens in a new window\nExecutive Branch\nThis link opens in a new window\nLaws, Regulations, Constitutions\nThis link opens in a new window\n<<\nPrevious:\nDepartments and Agencies\nNext:\nExecutive Branch Documents >>\nLast Updated:\nOct 16, 2026 1:35 PM\nURL:\nhttps://guides.lib.uw.edu/research/federal\nPrint Page\nLogin to LibApps\nSubjects:\nGovernment Publications\nPolitical Science, Government & Law\nTags:\nfederal_government\nReport a problem.\nUniversity of Washington Libraries\nBox 352900 Seattle, WA 98195-2900 USA\nphone: 206-543-0242 -\nContact Us\nUW Home\n/\nPrivacy\n/\nTerms\n/\nReport a Barrier\nText on this page created by UW Libraries is licensed under a CC BY-NC 4.0 license.\nImages and video are not included.\nSee details\n.",
