          "page_text": "Euro Area Stock Market Index (EU50) - Quote - Chart - Historical Data - News | Trading Economics\nCalendar\nNews\nMarkets\nCommodities\nIndexes\nShares\nCurrencies\nCrypto\nBonds\nEarnings\nIndicators\nCountries\nForecasts\nCommodities\nIndexes\nCurrencies\nCrypto\nBonds\nCountries\nIndicators\nMembers\nDATA PLANS\nDownload historical data for 20 million indicators using your browser.\nSubscription Plans\n|\nFeatures\nAPI GATEWAY\nDirect access to our data from your apps using any programing language.\nFeatures\n|\nDocs\n|\nDeveloper\nAlready a user?\nLogin\nCalendar\nNews\nMarkets\nCommodities\nIndexes\nShares\nCurrencies\nCrypto\nBonds\nEarnings\nHolidays\nCountries\nUnited States\nUnited Kingdom\nEuro Area\nAustralia\nCanada\nJapan\nChina\nBrazil\nRussia\nIndia\nMore Countries\nIndicators\nInterest Rate\nInflation Rate\nUnemployment Rate\nGDP Growth\nGDP per Capita\nCurrent Account\nGold Reserves\nGovernment Debt\nCrude Oil Production\nGasoline Prices\nCredit Rating\nMore Indicators\nForecasts\nMarkets & Indicators\nCountries\nCountries\nIndicators\nCommodities\nIndexes\nCurrencies\nCrypto\nBonds\nSolutions\nData Subscriptions\nAPI Access\nPricing\nApps\nApp Store\nGoogle Play\nTwitter\nAbout\nCustomers\nInvestors\nCareers\nAbout us\nContact\nEuro Area Stock Market Index (EU50)\nQuote - Chart - Historical Data - News\nSummary\nStats\nForecast\nAlerts\nActual\n6099\nDaily Change\n-127.50\n-2.05%\nMonthly\n-3.18%\nYearly\n7.60%\nQ4 Forecast\n6070\nEuro Area Stock Market Index (EU50) - Summary\nEuropean stocks fell sharply on Thursday, with the Stoxx 50 dropping around 2% to a more than four-month low and the broader Stoxx 600 losing 1% to a one-week low. Italy’s market declined over 2.5%, Spain fell around 2%, while Germany and France lost more than 1% and the UK slipped less than 0.5%. Basic resources plunged over 3.5% as metals prices tumbled, including silver down nearly 5%, copper over 2.5% and gold more than 2%. Banks fell over 3%, while industrial goods and services lost around 2.5% and technology declined 2%. Energy and travel stocks also weakened. In contrast, health care and food, beverage & tobacco each gained around 2%. Among individual stocks, Hermès dropped 5% after reporting flat sales, while Thales and Barclays declined despite raising its outlook and reporting higher profits, respectively. SAP rose over 3.5% after upgrading its full-year outlook, while Nestlé gained 2.3% on strong quarterly sales. Roche was little changed despite better-than-expected sales.\nEuro Area Stock Market Index (EU50) - Stats\nEuro Area's main stock market index, the EU50, fell to 6099 points on October 22, 2026, losing 1.84% from the previous session. Over the past month, the index has declined 3.18%, though it remains 7.60% higher than a year ago, according to trading on a contract for difference (CFD) that tracks this benchmark index from Euro Area.  Historically, the Euro Area Stock Market Index (EU50) reached an all time high of 6582.30 in August of 2026. Euro Area Stock Market Index (EU50) - data, forecasts, historical chart - was last updated on  October 22 of 2026.\nEuro Area Stock Market Index (EU50) - Forecast\nEuro Area's main stock market index, the EU50, fell to 6099 points on October 22, 2026, losing 1.84% from the previous session. Over the past month, the index has declined 3.18%, though it remains 7.60% higher than a year ago, according to trading on a contract for difference (CFD) that tracks this benchmark index from Euro Area. The Euro Area Stock Market Index (EU50) is expected to trade at 6069.66 points by the end of this quarter, according to Trading Economics global macro models and analysts expectations. Looking forward, we estimate it to trade at 5585.68 in 12 months time.\nIndexes\nPrice\nDay\nMonth\nYear\nDate\nFR40\n7,811.26\n-110.42\n-1.39%\n-3.84%\n-5.04%\nOct/22\nDE40\n25,371.75\n-273.29\n-1.07%\n-0.15%\n4.81%\nOct/22\nIT40\n48,383.66\n-1,260.03\n-2.54%\n-6.93%\n14.16%\nOct/22\nES35\n18,913.50\n-410.00\n-2.12%\n-3.66%\n19.77%\nOct/22\nEU100\n1,844.41\n-30.20\n-1.61%\n-2.79%\n7.60%\nOct/22\nEU600\n637.74\n-6.75\n-1.05%\n-0.34%\n11.02%\nOct/22\nEU50\n6,099.30\n-127.50\n-2.05%\n-3.18%\n7.60%\nOct/22\nComponents\nPrice\nDay\nYear\nMCap\nDate\nASML Holding\n1,419.60\n-74.20\n-4.97%\n59.92%\n655.71B\nOct/22\nSAP\n215.35\n6.70\n3.21%\n-11.01%\n271.88B\nOct/22\nSiemens\n255.70\n-11.05\n-4.14%\n6.70%\n257.78B\nOct/22\nLinde\n453.00\n8.20\n1.84%\n16.93%\n256.46B\nOct/22\nL'Oréal\n413.00\n7.30\n1.80%\n10.06%\n247.9B\nOct/22\nLVMH\n387.80\n-0.80\n-0.21%\n-37.53%\n217.87B\nOct/22\nBanco Santander\n11.48\n-0.49\n-4.06%\n36.81%\n215.22B\nOct/22\nInditex\n56.24\n-0.56\n-0.99%\n15.29%\n198.84B\nOct/22\nAllianz\n432.60\n-4.20\n-0.96%\n23.53%\n193.96B\nOct/22\nSchneider Electric\n235.75\n-13.35\n-5.36%\n-5.36%\n188.54B\nOct/22\nRelated\nLast\nPrevious\nUnit\nReference\nEuro Area Inflation Rate\n3.80\n3.20\npercent\nSep 2026\nEuro Area Interest Rate\n2.65\n2.40\npercent\nSep 2026\nEuro Area Unemployment Rate\n6.40\n6.40\npercent\nAug 2026\nEuro Area Stock Market Index (EU50)\nThe EURO STOXX 50 is a major stock market index which tracks the performance of 50 Blue-chip companies based in twelve Euro Area countries: Austria, Belgium, Finland, France, Germany, Greece, Ireland, Italy, Luxembourg, the Netherlands, Portugal and Spain. Stock index prices displayed on Trading Economics are based on over-the-counter (OTC), contract for difference (CFD), perpetual futures, and other derivative financial instruments intended to provide a general market reference only. These prices do not represent official exchange-traded index values or official licensing data from exchanges or index providers. The data is supplied by third-party providers and may differ from official market prices, settlement values, or real-time exchange data. While efforts are made to ensure reliability, Trading Economics does not independently verify the data and makes no representations or warranties regarding its accuracy, completeness, or fitness for any particular purpose. Users seeking official real-time or settlement data should obtain it directly from the relevant exchanges or authorized market data providers.\nActual\nPrevious\nHighest\nLowest\nDates\nUnit\nFrequency\n6099.30\n6213.45\n6582.30\n615.90\n1986 - 2026\npoints\nDaily\nMarket Data Coverage:\nEuro Area\nNews Stream\nEuropean Stocks Sink on Thursday\nEuropean stocks fell sharply on Thursday, with the Stoxx 50 dropping around 2% to a more than four-month low and the broader Stoxx 600 losing 1% to a one-week low. Italy’s market declined over 2.5%, Spain fell around 2%, while Germany and France lost more than 1% and the UK slipped less than 0.5%. Basic resources plunged over 3.5% as metals prices tumbled, including silver down nearly 5%, copper over 2.5% and gold more than 2%. Banks fell over 3%, while industrial goods and services lost around 2.5% and technology declined 2%. Energy and travel stocks also weakened. In contrast, health care and food, beverage & tobacco each gained around 2%. Among individual stocks, Hermès dropped 5% after reporting flat sales, while Thales and Barclays declined despite raising its outlook and reporting higher profits, respectively. SAP rose over 3.5% after upgrading its full-year outlook, while Nestlé gained 2.3% on strong quarterly sales. Roche was little changed despite better-than-expected sales.\n2026-10-22\nEuropean Stocks Fall, STOXX 50 Near Mid-June Lows\nEuropean stocks fell on Thursday, with the STOXX 50 sliding 1.5% to its lowest levels since mid-June and the STOXX 600 declining 0.9%, as disappointing results from US megacap companies reignited concerns over AI-related valuations and fuelled a global equity sell-off. Investors also kept a close eye on quarterly earnings from European companies, while oil prices extended their gains amid renewed attacks on Saudi Arabian facilities. Hermes tumbled 6.6% after reporting no sales growth in the region encompassing China. Roche fell 1.3% despite posting stronger-than-expected sales, while Thales slipped 1.7% even after raising its full-year outlook. Barclays edged down 0.2% despite reporting a 12% year-on-year increase in profit. In contrast, SAP shares were little changed, with the company lifting its full-year outlook. Nestle gained 2.3% after reporting strong third-quarter sales growth, supported by robust demand for snacks and pet food in emerging markets.\n2026-10-22\nEuropean Stocks Head for Lower Open\nEuropean equity markets were poised to open lower on Thursday amid renewed selling in technology and artificial intelligence stocks fueled by persistent concerns over the sustainability of heavy AI investments. Sentiment was also pressured by rising oil prices following a deadly attack on a Saudi oil field, escalating geopolitical tensions in the Middle East and intensifying inflationary concerns. Investors across Europe are also monitoring Eurozone new car registrations, French business confidence data, and the Turkish central bank’s latest policy decision. On the corporate front, Nestlé and Roche reported strong third-quarter sales growth, while SAP posted faster-than-expected expansion in its cloud business. In premarket trading, Euro Stoxx 50 futures fell 1%, while Stoxx 600 futures declined 0.6%.\n2026-10-22\nRelated\nEuropean Stocks Sink on Thursday\nEurozone Consumer Confidence Falls More than Expected\nEuro Weakens Amid French Fiscal Concerns\nEurozone Investor Morale Falls to 4-Month Low\nEurozone Construction Output Expands at Fastest Pace in 10 Months\nEurozone Current Account Surplus Narrows Sharply in August\nEuro Area Posts Widest Trade Deficit in Over 3 Years\nEuro Area Inflation Rate Confirmed at 3-Year High in September\nEuro Area Industrial Output Falls for 3rd Month\nECB Keeps Rate Path Open Amid Inflation Uncertainty: Minutes\nLatest\nBrent Tops $113 as Middle East Tensions Escalate\nOil Tops $99 as Middle East Tensions Escalate\nArgentina’s Retail Sales Surge in August\nArgentina’s Consumer Confidence Rises in October\nCanadian Raw Materials Inflation Exceeds Forecasts\nCanada’s New Home Prices Fall in September\nCAC 40 Falls Amid Fiscal Concerns and Mixed Earnings\nCanada Producer Prices Rise the Most in 6 Months\nUS Mortgage Rates See First Drop in 9 Weeks\nDAX Closes in the Red\nCommodity\nIndex\nStocks\nForex\nCrypto\nBond\nActual\nChg\n%Chg\nCrude Oil\n99.261\n2.11\n2.17%\nBrent\n113.016\n4.20\n3.86%\nNatural gas\n2.7842\n0.01\n-0.21%\nGasoline\n3.1744\n0.05\n1.73%\nHeating Oil\n4.9538\n0.19\n4.00%\nGold\n4021.70\n119.40\n-2.88%\nSilver\n54.530\n3.43\n-5.91%\nCopper\n6.3659\n0.18\n-2.71%\nSoybeans\n1253.50\n8.25\n0.66%\nWheat\n624.75\n3.50\n-0.56%\nCoal\n150.55\n0.10\n-0.07%\nSteel\n3056.00\n24.00\n-0.78%\nEU Gas\n90.85\n3.43\n3.92%\nLumber\n523.52\n2.02\n0.39%\nMore\nActual\nChg\n%Chg\nEURUSD\n1.12123\n0.0020\n-0.18%\nGBPUSD\n1.33929\n0.0001\n-0.01%\nAUDUSD\n0.70167\n0.0012\n-0.18%\nNZDUSD\n0.56810\n0.0019\n-0.33%\nUSDJPY\n156.890\n1.2495\n-0.79%\nUSDCNY\n6.67324\n0.0016\n-0.02%\nUSDCHF\n0.82067\n0.0037\n-0.45%\nUSDCAD\n1.40110\n0.0036\n-0.25%\nUSDMXN\n18.7130\n0.0273\n0.15%\nUSDINR\n95.0800\n0.6431\n0.68%\nUSDBRL\n5.04110\n0.0169\n0.34%\nUSDRUB\n81.9000\n0.0231\n-0.03%\nUSDKRW\n1326.02\n1.1500\n-0.09%\nDXY\n101.662\n0.1183\n-0.12%\nUSDTRY\n49.6258\n0.0710\n0.14%\nMore\nActual\nChg\n%Chg\nUS500\n7663.13\n94\n-1.21%\nUS30\n52839\n17\n-0.03%\nUS100\n28958\n865\n-2.90%\nJP225\n62460\n2,161\n-3.34%\nGB100\n10725\n28\n-0.26%\nDE40\n25372\n273\n-1.07%\nFR40\n7811\n110\n-1.39%\nIT40\n48384\n1,260\n-2.54%\nES35\n18914\n410\n-2.12%\nASX200\n8793\n38\n-0.43%\nSHANGHAI\n3658\n55\n-1.48%\nSENSEX\n73249\n460\n-0.62%\nTSX\n35973\n352\n-0.97%\nMOEX\n2427\n21\n-0.86%\nIBOVESPA\n203815\n2,680\n-1.30%\nMore\nActual\nChg\n%Chg\nApple\n348.30\n4.31\n1.25%\nTesla\n375.42\n11.05\n-2.86%\nMicrosoft\n520.87\n12.08\n-2.27%\nAmazon\n250.25\n12.12\n-4.62%\nMeta\n648.49\n49.03\n-7.03%\nNvidia\n212.07\n9.90\n-4.46%\nVisa\n414.17\n