endently verify the data and makes no representations or warranties regarding its accuracy, completeness, or fitness for any particular purpose. Users seeking official real-time or settlement data should obtain it directly from the relevant exchanges or authorized market data providers.\nActual\nPrevious\nHighest\nLowest\nDates\nUnit\nFrequency\n7666.37\n7757.17\n7844.67\n4.40\n1928 - 2026\npoints\nDaily\nMarket Data Coverage:\nUnited States\nNews Stream\nWall Street Falls as Tech Sell-Off Deepens\nUS stock indices closed lower on Thursday amid a sell-off in technology stocks. The S&P 500 fell 1.2%,  and the Nasdaq lost 2.9%, while the Dow closed flat. Tech stocks were under pressure as corporate earnings failed to impress, renewing concerns over the AI trade, while the rotation away from semiconductor stocks continued. Meta sank 7% despite reporting higher earnings and revenue, as its credit risk reached a record high. Meta plans to ramp up capital spending reignited fears that tech firms will take on more debt to finance AI infrastructure. Other hyperscalers also declined, with Alphabet (-3.2%), Microsoft (-2.2%) and Amazon (-4.6%) falling ahead of their earnings reports next week. Tesla dropped 3.1% after scaling back its spending plans, with capital investment in the previous quarter coming in below expectations. Lam Research tumbled 11.8% after issuing a disappointing outlook. Among chipmakers, Nvidia lost 4.4%, Micron shed 6.3%, AMD plunged 8.1%, and Intel fell 8%.\n2026-10-22\nThe Dow Jones Index Opens 0.33% Lower\nThe Dow Jones Index is losing 174 points. Losses were led by Honeywell International (-3.52%), Caterpillar (-3.26%) and Amazon (-2.37%). Offsetting the fall, top gainers were P&G (5.49%), J&J (1.27%) and Salesforce (1.14%).\n2026-10-22\nUS Stocks Lower, Meta Sinks\nUS stocks fell on Thursday, with the S&P 500 down 1.3%, the Nasdaq losing 2.2% and the Dow Jones declining nearly 110 points. Tech stocks remained under pressure as corporate earnings failed to impress investors, fueling renewed concerns over the AI trade, while the rotation away from semiconductor stocks continued. Communication services and consumer discretionary were also under heavy pressure while consumer staples outperformed. Meanwhile, oil prices climbed amid renewed supply risks and escalating geopolitical tensions. Meta tumbled 5.8% despite reporting higher earnings and revenue, while Tesla fell 0.8% after scaling back its spending plans, as capital investment in the previous quarter came in below expectations. Lam Research tumbled more than 10% after issuing a disappointing outlook. Nvidia dropped 3.9%, while IBM lost 1.3%. In addition, Honeywell fell 0.1% despite raising its full-year outlook. In contrast, Lockheed Martin gained 6.6% after beating on earnings and revenue.\n2026-10-22\nRelated\nComfort Systems USA earnings above expectations at 13.91 USD\nNewmont Mining earnings below expectations at 2.20 USD\nWall Street Falls as Tech Sell-Off Deepens\nBloom Energy Corp earnings above expectations at 0.94 USD\nWestern Digital earnings above expectations at 4.64 USD\nUS Mortgage Rates See First Drop in 9 Weeks\nKansas Fed Manufacturing Activity Growth Slows\nUS Natural Gas Stocks Rise Less Than Expected\nDollar Little Changed at 3-Week Low\nUS 10-Year Treasury Yield Falls to 5%\nLatest\nComfort Systems USA earnings above expectations at 13.91 USD\nNewmont Mining earnings below expectations at 2.20 USD\nWall Street Falls as Tech Sell-Off Deepens\nBloom Energy Corp earnings above expectations at 0.94 USD\nWestern Digital earnings above expectations at 4.64 USD\nBrent Tops $113 as Middle East Tensions Escalate\nOil Tops $99 as Middle East Tensions Escalate\nArgentina’s Retail Sales Surge in August\nArgentina’s Consumer Confidence Rises in October\nCanadian Raw Materials Inflation Exceeds Forecasts\nCommodity\nIndex\nStocks\nForex\nCrypto\nBond\nActual\nChg\n%Chg\nCrude Oil\n99.203\n2.05\n2.11%\nBrent\n112.916\n4.10\n3.76%\nNatural gas\n2.779